\section{Introduction}\label{sec:causal-intro}

Performance profiling is one of the most important tools in the compiler
developer's and systems programmer's arsenal.  By measuring where a program
spends its time, profiling guides optimization decisions: which functions to
inline, which loops to vectorize, which data structures to restructure.

Traditional profiling tools---sampling profilers, call-graph profilers, and
hardware performance counters---tell us \emph{where} a program spends its time.
But they do not tell us \emph{how much it would help} to optimize a given
location.  A function that consumes 30\% of the execution time might seem like
an attractive optimization target, but if optimizing it would cause other parts
of the program to become the bottleneck, the actual speedup may be far less than
expected.

\emph{Causal profiling}~\cite{curtsinger2013codepet} answers a different
question: \emph{``What would be the effect of speeding up this specific piece of
code by $x$\%?''}  By performing virtual speedup experiments---artificially
slowing down all other code to simulate the effect of speeding up the target
code---causal profiling reveals the \emph{causal} relationship between
optimization efforts and performance improvements.

This chapter develops the theory and practice of causal profiling
(\cref{sec:causal-concept}), its implementation (\cref{sec:causal-impl}), its
application to what-if analysis (\cref{sec:causal-whatif}), and its integration
with compiler optimization (\cref{sec:causal-integration}).

% ============================================================
% Traditional Profiling Limitations
% ============================================================
\section{Traditional Profiling Limitations}\label{sec:causal-traditional}

\subsection{Sampling Profilers}\label{sec:causal-sampling}

\emph{Sampling profilers} periodically interrupt the program and record the
current program counter.  The resulting profile shows the distribution of time
across functions and code locations.

\begin{definition}[Sampling Profile]\label{def:sampling-profile}
A \emph{sampling profile} is a mapping $P : \mathit{Loc} \to \mathbb{N}$ where
$P(l)$ is the number of samples that landed at location $l$.  The \emph{self
time} of $l$ is $P(l) / N$ where $N$ is the total number of samples.
\end{definition}

\subsection{The Attribution Problem}\label{sec:causal-attribution}

Traditional profiles suffer from the \emph{attribution problem}: the time
attributed to a function may not reflect the potential benefit of optimizing it.

\begin{example}[Attribution Problem]\label{ex:attribution}
Consider a program with two phases: initialization (10\% of time) and
computation (90\% of time).  Within computation, function $A$ takes 50\% and
function $B$ takes 40\%.  A traditional profile shows $A$ as the hottest
function.  But if $A$ is memory-bound (waiting for data) and $B$ is
compute-bound, optimizing $B$ may yield a larger speedup than optimizing $A$.
\end{example}

\subsection{Amdahl's Law and Its Limitations}\label{sec:causal-amdahl}

\emph{Amdahl's law} provides a theoretical upper bound on speedup:
\[
S(N) = \frac{1}{(1 - p) + p/N}
\]
where $p$ is the fraction of execution time that can be parallelized (or
optimized) and $N$ is the speedup factor for that fraction.

While Amdahl's law is correct, it requires knowing $p$---the fraction of time
that would actually benefit from the optimization.  Traditional profilers do not
directly provide this information.

