\subsection{Implementation}\label{sec:causal-impl}

Causal profiling is implemented by:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Identifying the target:} Select a code location $l$ (e.g., a
      function or a call site).
\item \textbf{Inserting delays:} At every \emph{other} code location, insert
      delays that slow execution.  The delay at each location is proportional to
      the time spent there.
\item \textbf{Measuring:} Run the program with the delays and measure the total
      execution time.  The difference from the baseline gives the causal effect.
\item \textbf{Varying the speedup:} Repeat for different virtual speedup
      percentages (10\%, 20\%, \ldots, 100\%) to produce a curve.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Delay Injection}\label{sec:causal-delay}

The key implementation challenge is injecting delays without significantly
distorting the program's behavior.  \textsc{CodePet}~\cite{curtsinger2013codepet}
uses two approaches:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Thread-based delays:} For multi-threaded programs, delay threads
      that are not executing the target code.  This works because threads that
      are not making progress do not affect the program's critical path.
\item \textbf{Signal-based delays:} For single-threaded programs, use timer
      signals to periodically pause execution outside the target code.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Correctness of Causal Profiling}\label{sec:causal-correctness}

\begin{theorem}[Causal Profiling Correctness]\label{thm:causal-correct}
If a virtual speedup of $s\%$ at location $l$ results in a measured program
speedup of $r\%$, then actually speeding up $l$ by $s\%$ would result in a
program speedup of approximately $r\%$, assuming:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The program's behavior is deterministic.
\item The delays do not change the program's scheduling or synchronization
      behavior.
\item The speedup at $l$ does not change the program's control flow.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{caution}
Causal profiling makes assumptions that may not hold for all programs.
Non-deterministic programs, programs with complex synchronization, and programs
where optimization changes control flow may produce misleading causal profiles.
The results should be interpreted as \emph{estimates} rather than guarantees.
\end{caution}

