\section{Performance Experiments}\label{sec:causal-experiments}

\subsection{Designing Experiments}\label{sec:causal-design}

A causal profiling experiment consists of:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Target selection:} Which code locations to profile.
\item \textbf{Speedup range:} The range of virtual speedups to test (typically
      0--100\%).
\item \textbf{Workload:} The input(s) to the program.
\item \textbf{Measurement:} How to measure the program's performance
      (throughput, latency, etc.).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Interpreting Results}\label{sec:causal-interpret}

The output of causal profiling is a set of curves, one per code location,
showing the program speedup as a function of the virtual speedup at that
location.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Steep curve:} Optimizing this location yields large benefits.
      This is a good optimization target.
\item \textbf{Flat curve:} Optimizing this location yields little benefit.
      The bottleneck is elsewhere.
\item \textbf{Concave curve:} The benefit decreases as the speedup increases,
      indicating that other bottlenecks emerge.
\item \textbf{Linear curve:} The benefit scales proportionally with the speedup.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Comparison with Traditional Profiling}\label{sec:causal-comparison}

