\section{What-If Analysis}\label{sec:causal-whatif}

\subsection{Hypothetical Optimizations}\label{sec:causal-hypothetical}

Causal profiling enables \emph{what-if analysis}: asking ``what if I could make
this code $x$\% faster?'' without actually implementing the optimization.  This
is valuable for prioritizing optimization efforts:

\begin{itemize}
\item ``What if I could vectorize this loop?'' (virtual speedup: 4--8$\times$)
\item ``What if I could eliminate this lock?'' (virtual speedup: 100\% for the
      critical section)
\item ``What if I could improve cache hit rate by 50\%?'' (virtual speedup
      proportional to memory latency reduction)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Cost--Benefit Analysis}\label{sec:causal-cost-benefit}

By combining causal profiling with estimates of optimization cost, developers
can perform \emph{cost--benefit analysis}:

\[
\mathit{ROI} = \frac{\mathit{expected\_speedup} \times \mathit{usage\_frequency}}{\mathit{optimization\_cost}}
\]

Causal profiling provides the $\mathit{expected\_speedup}$ component; the
developer estimates the cost and usage frequency.

