\section{Tools and Methodologies}\label{sec:causal-tools}

\subsection{CodePet}\label{sec:causal-codepet}

\textsc{CodePet}~\cite{curtsinger2013codepet} is the original causal profiling
tool.  It works with multi-threaded C/C++ programs, using thread-based delay
injection to perform virtual speedup experiments.

\subsection{Other Approaches}\label{sec:causal-other}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Perf} (Linux): Traditional sampling profiler; can be combined
      with causal analysis.
\item \textbf{Intel VTune}: Hardware performance counter profiling; provides
      detailed micro-architectural analysis.
\item \textbf{Coz}~\cite{curtsinger2015coz}: An improved causal profiler that
      uses causal invariants to reduce the overhead of delay injection.
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
Causal profiling was introduced by Curtsinger and Berger~\cite{curtsinger2013codepet}
in 2013 with the \textsc{CodePet} tool.  The follow-up work \textsc{Coz}
(Causal Profiler)~\cite{curtsinger2015coz} improved the technique by using
\emph{causal invariants}---program points whose execution count is invariant
under the virtual speedup---to reduce the overhead and improve the accuracy of
causal profiles.  Causal profiling represents a shift in performance analysis
from observational to interventional methods, drawing on ideas from causal
inference in statistics and the potential outcomes framework.
\end{historical}

% ============================================================
% Theoretical Foundations
% ============================================================
\section{Theoretical Foundations}\label{sec:causal-theory}

\subsection{Causal Inference and Performance}\label{sec:causal-inference}

Causal profiling draws on the framework of \emph{causal inference} from
statistics and epidemiology.  The key concept is the \emph{potential outcomes}
framework (also known as the Rubin causal model):

\begin{definition}[Potential Outcomes]\label{def:potential-outcomes}
For a program $P$ and a code location $l$, let $T(P, l, s)$ be the execution
time of $P$ when $l$ is sped up by $s\%$.  The \emph{causal effect} of
optimizing $l$ by $s\%$ is:
\[
\Delta(P, l, s) = T(P, l, 0) - T(P, l, s)
\]
The \emph{fundamental problem of causal inference} is that we can never observe
both $T(P, l, 0)$ and $T(P, l, s)$ for the same execution---we must estimate
the counterfactual.
\end{definition}

Causal profiling estimates the counterfactual by using virtual speedup: slowing
down all other code to simulate the effect of speeding up $l$.

\subsection{Connection to Amdahl's Law}\label{sec:causal-amdahl-connection}

The causal profile curve for a location $l$ is related to Amdahl's law.  If $p$
is the fraction of execution time that is truly dependent on $l$, then the
causal profile follows:
\[
\mathit{speedup}(s) = \frac{1}{(1 - p) + p / (1 + s/100)}
\]
Fitting this curve to the observed causal profile yields an estimate of $p$,
the \emph{optimization potential} of location $l$.

\subsection{Limitations}\label{sec:causal-limitations}

Causal profiling has several theoretical limitations:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Non-compositionality:} The combined effect of optimizing two
      locations may differ from the sum of their individual effects (due to
      interactions and shifting bottlenecks).
\item \textbf{Scheduling sensitivity:} The results may depend on the OS
      scheduler, especially for multi-threaded programs.
\item \textbf{Phase behavior:} Programs with distinct phases may have different
      causal profiles in different phases.
\end{itemize}

% ============================================================
% Summary
% ============================================================
\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:causal-summary}

Causal profiling provides a principled approach to performance analysis that
directly answers the question developers care about: ``What should I optimize?''
By performing virtual speedup experiments, causal profiling reveals the causal
relationship between local optimizations and global performance improvements.

Key open challenges include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Scalability:} Reducing the overhead of causal profiling for large
      programs.
\item \textbf{Non-determinism:} Handling programs with non-deterministic behavior.
\item \textbf{Compiler integration:} Automatically using causal profiles to
      guide optimization decisions.
\item \textbf{Multi-dimensional optimization:} Extending causal profiling to
      handle trade-offs between multiple objectives (time, energy, memory).
\end{itemize}
